  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\n     OVERVIEW THROUGH NEXT THURSDAY...\n\nEarly this afternoon, water vapor satellite and upper air analysis \nshow strong mid/upper ridging across the southwestern CONUS with \nbroad and deep troughing across the Great Lakes and northeast CONUS. \nThis places northwest upper flow over the northern/central plains. \nThe pattern will remain quite stagnant over the next 7 days with \nonly very subtle variations in the strength and placement of the \nsouthwest CONUS ridge from day to day. Through mid-week next week, \nsubtle perturbations embedded within the aforementioned northwest \nflow are forecast to sweep across the central plains each night and \nearly morning. The exact track of these waves will be determined by \nhow much the aforementioned mid/upper ridge has shifted into the \ncentral plains from day to day. Needless to say, the confidence in \nthe precipitation forecast over the next week is fairly low. Still \nmodel guidance suggest there will be at least some rain and storm \nchances each night and early morning from tonight through Wednesday \nnight/Thursday morning.\n\n     STORM CHANCES TONIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY MORNING...\n\nLet's take a deeper dive over the next couple of days or so where \nconfidence in the upcoming forecast is a little higher. For this \nafternoon into late tonight, the latest CAM guidance has \nsignificantly backed off on showing a complex of storms impacting \nportions of northwest and central Kansas. This makes sense given \nwarm mid-level temperatures supporting widespread convective \ninhibition across these areas. However, elevated parcels \novernight will generally be uncapped across much of the \nforecast area. Short term model guidance is honing in on a \nregion, generally across the Flint Hills, where an axis of 700 \nmb WAA is likely overnight tonight into Friday morning. MUCAPE \nis likely to remain well below 1000 J/kg, but effective shear \naround 40-50 knots could lead to some organized convection, and \nup to dime size hail is possible with elevated storms that \ndevelop overnight into Friday morning across the Flint Hills.\n\nShort term guidance appears to be more aggressive with showers and \nstorms Friday night into Saturday morning, particularly along and \neast of the I-135 corridor. Several models indicate a cluster of \nstorms developing in Nebraska and northern Kansas during the evening \nhours ahead of a sharp shortwave trough. These storms are likely to \ncongeal and progress south and east through the nighttime hours of \nFriday and into the early morning hours of Saturday. While storms \nare surface based Friday night across southern Nebraska and northern \nKansas, damaging winds with gusts around 60-75 mph are possible. \nHowever, as storms continue south and east, it's likely they will \nbecome more elevated thus reducing the damaging wind threat. That \nbeing said, stronger updrafts embedded within the cluster of storm \ncould produce up to ping pong ball size hail given 1500-2000 J/kg \nMUCAPE and over 60 knots of effective shear. Of course, there is the \npossibility that storms fail to develop, or coverage is more \nisolated across Nebraska and northern Kansas Friday evening and \nnight than currently forecast. If this scenario were to play out, \nit's unlikely much will impact the forecast area Friday night into \nearly Saturday morning.\n\n     TEMPERATURE FORECAST THROUGH NEXT THURSDAY...\n\nAs mentioned previously, a strong mid/upper ridge will remained \ncentered generally across the southwest CONUS over the next 7 days. \nMinor variations in where the ridge is located, along with potential \nleftover cloud cover from shower and thunderstorm activity each \nmorning will drive afternoon highs each day across the forecast \narea. In general, afternoon highs will be slightly above average for \nthis time of year with highs in the 90s each day. Additionally, lows \nwill generally be in the upper 60s to low 70s each night. All in \nall, while temperatures will be slightly above average, it won't be \nnearly as hot as it has been the past 2 to 3 weeks.",
